Spec: Architecture

Purpose:
  The lightweight Clean Architecture (Ports and Adapters) convention followed by every feature package under
  ./ (`src/aikana/`). It keeps FastHTML routes, rendered views and the SQLite adapter at the edges, and keeps domain
  rules and use cases in plain Python modules with no framework imports. This spec is the shared contract that every
  feature package's own spec references instead of restating.

Structure:
  domain.py: plain dataclasses and business rules for the package's entity or entities; no imports beyond the
    standard library.
  ports.py: `typing.Protocol` interfaces that `services.py` depends on for persistence or other external effects;
    depends only on domain.py and `typing`.
  services.py: application use cases that orchestrate domain.py through ports.py; the only business-logic layer.
  repository_sqlite.py: the adapter that implements ports.py using `fastlite`'s MiniDataAPI Spec; translates between
    SQLite rows and domain.py dataclasses.
  view.py: pure rendering functions that turn domain objects or view-models into FastHTML FT components.
  routes.py: the package's inbound adapter; the only module allowed to import both FastHTML and the package's
    services.py, and the place where a feature's routes are registered on the app.
  A feature package owns only the files it needs; a view-only feature may have no domain.py, ports.py or
    repository_sqlite.py, and a storage-only feature may have no view.py or routes.py.

Must:
  Persistence uses `fastlite`, which implements the MiniDataAPI Spec, as the sole data-access layer. `fastlite` is
  declared as an explicit dependency in `pyproject.toml` (it is only a `dev` extra of `python-fasthtml`, not a
  transitive runtime dependency, so it must be added directly).
  Each feature's domain.py dataclasses are distinct from any table-bound dataclass that `fastlite` produces;
  repository_sqlite.py converts between the two so persistence shape never leaks into domain.py or services.py.
  ./shared/db.py owns the single `fastlite` `Database` connection opened at `/data/app.db`; every
  repository_sqlite.py imports it rather than opening its own connection.
  Each repository_sqlite.py creates its own table on first use, idempotently, through the shared connection.
  Dependencies point inward only: routes.py depends on services.py and view.py; services.py depends on domain.py and
  ports.py; repository_sqlite.py depends on domain.py and ports.py; domain.py and ports.py depend on nothing local.
  Cross-feature use goes through a dependency's services.py or ports.py, never through another feature's
  repository_sqlite.py or domain.py directly.
  A reference from one feature's entity to another feature's entity (for example CourseRealization's reference to
  a Course or a Semester) is stored as a plain id in domain.py, never as an imported object; only services.py may
  import the referenced feature's ports.py to validate that id.
  Two feature packages' Depends on may be mutual when one's view.py or services.py reads the other while the other
  only validates an id through the first's ports.py in its own services.py; this is not a circular import as long
  as domain.py and ports.py never depend on another feature, per the rule above.
  main.py is the composition root: it builds the shared `Database`, constructs each feature's repository and
  service, and passes the constructed services into each feature's routes.py when registering routes on the app.
  main.py constructs services with no cross-feature dependency first, then services that depend on those. A
  genuine mutual pair of services (one where each side's constructor would need the other) is wired in two
  phases: construct one side with its cross-feature dependency omitted, construct the other side with the first
  instance, then set the omitted dependency as a plain attribute on the first instance once both exist. Each
  side type-hints the other using a `TYPE_CHECKING`-guarded import so the classes never import each other at
  runtime, avoiding a real circular import.

Must not:
  domain.py and services.py must not import `fasthtml`, `starlette` or `fastlite`.
  view.py must not import repository_sqlite.py or open a database connection.
  routes.py must not query the database directly; it must call services.py.

Depends on:
  `fastlite`, an explicit `pyproject.toml` dependency, implementing the MiniDataAPI Spec, for all SQLite access.

Example:
  ports.py: `class CourseRepository(Protocol): def list(self) -> list[Course]: ...`
  services.py: `class CourseService: def __init__(self, repo: CourseRepository) -> None: self.repo = repo`
  repository_sqlite.py: `class SqliteCourseRepository: def list(self) -> list[Course]: return [to_domain(r) for r in table()]`
  routes.py: `@rt.get("/courses") def courses_index(): return view.course_list(service.list_courses())`
  A genuine mutual pair, wired in main.py: `realization_service = RealizationService(realization_repo, ...)`
  (constructed without a `semester_service`), then `semester_service = SemesterService(semester_repo, ...,
  realization_service)`, then `realization_service.semester_service = semester_service`. Each service module
  type-hints the other under `if TYPE_CHECKING:` so neither imports the other at runtime.
